diff --git a/docs/development/standalone-lease-baseline.md b/docs/development/standalone-lease-baseline.md
index e3047cd..668a2f4 100644
--- a/docs/development/standalone-lease-baseline.md
+++ b/docs/development/standalone-lease-baseline.md
@@ -64,18 +64,31 @@ It describes the pinned upstream, not completed standalone support.
   release, force-release, and status currently invoke global expiry helpers in
   `sqlitestore/claims.go` and `pgstore/claims_core.go`. Merely changing the
   background sweeper does not protect cached spoke claims in a mixed database.
-  Add backend conformance coverage and a separate focused fix before treating
-  standalone/spoke isolation as complete.
+  The revised K2 scopes each operation's opportunity expiry to the resolved
+  issue's project, with shared backend conformance coverage, before enabling
+  standalone routes. Storage does not infer authority; callers remain
+  responsible for never applying authoritative operations to spoke caches.
 - **Guard identity must be exact.** Acquire/renew/release compare holder,
   holder instance, and client kind. The legacy mutation gate can compare less.
   Renew/release have no expected-ClaimUID parameter, making per-tenure principal
   uniqueness necessary even after strict close is added.
 - **Read-only status is not proof of liveness.** An expired cached row can still
   have Held=true. Guard validation must check actual expiry in the transaction.
-- **Protocol capability matters.** Existing close-v1 negotiation does not prove
-  that an older server enforces the new header. Strict clients must fail closed
-  against unsupported servers. Do not deploy the new precondition before its
-  idempotency fingerprint support lands.
+- **Protocol version matters.** Revised K6 introduces close-v2 together with
+  the exact lease guard. A lease header without close-v2 must be rejected;
+  strict clients send both. Verify that old servers reject the unknown protocol
+  enum rather than silently ignoring the header. Capability discovery is not
+  the correctness boundary. K7 fingerprints protocol and ClaimUID, preserving
+  receipt-before-guard replay and close-v1 compatibility. Do not deploy the new
+  precondition before its idempotency fingerprint support lands.
+- **Claim attempts survive uncertain responses, not runtime replacement.**
+  F4/F5 generate and save one execution ID before sending a logical acquire;
+  network retries reuse it. Definitive denial/release/loss retires it. A new Pi
+  runtime must not restore it from conversation history, even with the same
+  session UUID. Waiting for the old lease's TTL is an intentional safety cost.
+- **Close principal must reuse the acquire identity seam.** K6 must derive the
+  same holder/instance/client-kind through a shared request-principal helper,
+  not reconstruct the host subject hash or substitute the display actor.
 - **SQLite embedding is single-owner.** Its idempotency lock is Store-local,
   unlike PostgreSQL advisory locks. Preserve the single-daemon file ownership
   assumption in the embedding host.
@@ -101,9 +114,45 @@ Kata workspace; issue tracking initialization remains a separate setup step.
 
 ## Delivery order
 
-K1 only classifies authority and preserves existing routes and errors. Then
-implement standalone actions, read projection, authoritative expiry, close-time
-release, typed strict precondition, retry fingerprint support, and terminology
-in independently tested commits. The opportunity-expiry fix above is an
-additional prerequisite for authoritative isolation. Begin the embedding host
-and Pi extension only after the Kata correctness work passes.
+K1 only classifies authority and preserves existing routes and errors. Revised
+K2 enables standalone actions together with target-project opportunity expiry;
+force-release retains its existing authentication and host authorization.
+An existing daemon test expected expiry in another project; this incidental
+side effect is deliberately removed. The rollback test instead uses a stale
+sibling in the target project so it still exercises actual expiry rollback.
+K3 changes read projection; K4 sweeps only authoritative projects; K5 extracts
+narrow close-time authoritative release without broadening federation audit;
+K6 adds close-v2 and the typed exact principal/ClaimUID guard; K7 hardens retry
+fingerprints and ambiguous commits. Keep these independently tested commits
+and do not deploy K6 without K7. Begin the embedding host and Pi extension only
+after the Kata correctness work passes.
+
+## K2 implementation verification
+
+Standalone acquire/renew/release/status/force-release now use local authority.
+Spoke forwarding and force-release authentication remain unchanged; spoke
+local force-release is refused. SQLite and PostgreSQL opportunity expiry uses
+only the resolved issue's project, including force-release.
+
+New tests first reproduced standalone HTTP 404 and cross-project expiry. The
+shared storage matrix covers standalone/hub × acquire/renew/release/force/status:
+expiry still occurs for a stale sibling in the target project, while both a
+spoke cache and an unrelated authoritative project's claim remain unchanged.
+Returned expiry events are checked against persisted events.
+
+Passed after implementation:
+
+```sh
+go test ./internal/daemon -count=1
+go test . -count=1
+go test ./internal/db/sqlitestore -count=1
+go test ./internal/db/pgstore -run 'TestStorageConformance|Claim' -count=1 -timeout=5m
+go build ./...
+git diff --check
+```
+
+The PostgreSQL conformance run used a real PostgreSQL backend. A separate attempt
+at the entire PostgreSQL package exceeded the shell's 600-second limit; no
+full-package PostgreSQL pass is claimed. Its conformance and claim tests were
+subsequently rerun successfully. No show hydration, sweeper, close, strict gate,
+violation audit, persisted schema, or CLI changes are included in K2.
